  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_80e7fade5f2e49539ba21d98be9fcaa9",
      "statement": "The optimizer maps each vault risk profile to an objective: CONSERVATIVE to global minimum variance, MODERATE and AGGRESSIVE to max-Sharpe tangency, and HYPER_RISKY to a max-expected-return linear program.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L374-L401",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e59fac0fe36a743c3bba650e489097f634871c0ad5e201311c1b968493f039c3:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_960da5145ea24f4c8bd641f4ba3c9d3d",
      "statement": "Every mean-variance objective is solved on the long-only unit simplex with a per-asset weight cap of 0.40 (0.60 for the hyper-risky profile) to prevent degenerate corner solutions, and falls back to equal weight when SLSQP fails or history is shorter than 20 bars.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L382-L401",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:416b04872ce79c2de97bc50631893873861a24d4f4f58f39475fcb282c2e4fde:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1fff758cbe864b9ebc0e7f089f4d19a6",
      "statement": "Global minimum variance serves the conservative profile specifically because it depends only on the covariance matrix and never on the noisy expected-return vector, making it the most estimation-robust objective available.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L403-L417",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d682340cbdd78cd196570f0dc6a448ac94cd5e40b9c4d284a827e8a8722a732d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_81c20146e66b4ab9a155cf1c2ea1ae8b",
      "statement": "Half-Kelly is the default sizing fraction and full Kelly is treated as an academic reference only, because full Kelly is acutely sensitive to estimation error in mu; Kelly is defined on excess returns, its output is clipped to [0,1] with no leverage, and a non-positive excess return returns zero.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L419-L443",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:33fd3316162fde88be08b43012cbe335de307e0fc880e9276e68b8cba89e10fa:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3519d0fb6f01475a85207897b60a9a0c",
      "statement": "In the multi-asset path Kelly is expressed as a risk-aversion-parameterised mean-variance objective where gamma=2 reproduces half-Kelly, gamma approaching 0 approaches full Kelly, and gamma approaching infinity collapses to minimum variance; baseline gammas run from 12.0 for fixed_income down to 1.5 for hyper_risky.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L445-L467",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8131b5ba1b991c70ff62deaf8ee108c9448da48ba3a3a466281513d037536637:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_6d5aef67562e4806bb887cb881df892a",
      "statement": "The regime-conditional multiplier scales effective risk aversion by 1.0x in risk_on and transition, 2.0x in risk_off, and 4.0x in crisis, so a moderate investor in a crisis regime operates at gamma=12.0 — fixed_income-equivalent — without changing their declared profile.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L468-L482",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:aa4509ab1752792f833ad34baa4327601594a40203017785815f19bf0221ac8e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_0a01118904444586a83d4a9831183e05",
      "statement": "The regime gamma multipliers are documented as a deliberately conservative engineering judgment rather than a claim of optimality, chosen below the 6-10x that research papers sometimes use in tail regimes in order to avoid whipsawing allocations on every regime flip.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L468-L482",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:aa4509ab1752792f833ad34baa4327601594a40203017785815f19bf0221ac8e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3f8af1091b3f47f38a94d3472722a3e2",
      "statement": "Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage is the production covariance estimator and its shrinkage intensity is derived analytically from the data rather than hand-tuned, with a fixed-intensity diagonal fallback at alpha=0.10 used only when the analytic estimator cannot run.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L527-L548",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:30ba283a6b8026bfb94588a6271ab8c50dcb94b4789395fd7d9f4d5018395474:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_259d7e2cef7d43e29fb92184e5293d59",
      "statement": "Hierarchical Risk Parity is documented as the principled diversification objective and the intended path for many-asset high-correlation baskets, but it is explicitly not what the production optimizer routes today — the five profiles go through GMV, max-Sharpe, and max-return with Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L484-L505",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:1fc0b1d910895ee3d8a60b91b071ee874c6a96663a61be19c9b87a12141cc2c8:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_b5e804581180406299bf8fb22d9484a3",
      "statement": "Black-Litterman is mapped onto the existing mu_override plus mu_shrinkage blending, which shrinks paper-extrapolated expected returns toward each asset's own sample mean at a default 0.5 blend so a single strategy's claimed CAGR is not promised across every asset it voted for.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L507-L525",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:82dd2e924f59ec2c7bd6b8210529213d46086aad6a886434f582d1ad1d229f53:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_f03cdeb9de804cbcbe0d4beae60cbcec",
      "statement": "Two reporting primitives make the optimizer legible: a per-asset marginal contribution to portfolio variance via the Euler decomposition that sums to 1, and closed-form one-year expected maximum drawdown (Magdon-Ismail & Atiya 2004) and parametric normal 95% VaR figures.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L550-L562",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:49032e9917c801d5541f37e6e00a419bd7a2510e117c04c2fbf57f2298164724:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3daaaa1222994776bee199eb5b1319b5",
      "statement": "The flat 5% risk-free constant is now only a fallback for the rigor statistics, which can instead be graded against the historical three-month T-bill series with the choice disclosed per result — but Kelly and the portfolio optimizer still take only the flat constant, so the claim that every excess-return figure is computed on the same basis is no longer accurate.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L586-L598",
